Binning is Sinning!
=======================

Group: Further Foundations

Open index.html directly (file://), retaining its sibling TeachingTools
directory, or serve them statically. The HTML lesson needs no R, Shiny,
WebR, external libraries, build, install or network. Original app.R and
model.R are preserved unchanged.

Lecture:
https://anguswright.github.io/LectureNotes/IntroductionToStatistics_Section1b_html.html

Explore
-------
Keep the observed stellar colours fixed while changing bin width
(0.1-1.5), bin origin (0-1 times the width) and Gaussian KDE bandwidth
(0.05-1). Sample size remains 50-1000. Undersmoothed, oversmoothed and
middle-ground presets, original guided examples and lecture link are
retained. An observation rug makes the fixed sample visible.

The known generating population is
  0.6*Normal(-0.8,0.35^2) + 0.4*Normal(0.9,0.35^2).
The teal curve is this truth, not a fit. A visible bump can reflect sample
noise, binning or smoothing rather than another astrophysical component.

Histogram heights are count/(n*bin width); all bins are in view and their
total area is 1. The Gaussian KDE is the direct normalized kernel sum:
  mean_i[normalPdf((x-x_i)/h)/h].
Its integral is 1 over the real line, not over the finite plotting window.
The outside-view metric sums Gaussian lower and upper tails directly to
avoid cancellation from subtracting a nearly-one mass from one. Extremely
small probabilities can still underflow to zero in floating-point math.

Intentional HTML differences
---------------------------
* Uses Astro.createRng(seed) rather than R's RNG: seeds preserve browser
  reproducibility, not identical R observations. A 1000-object cache is
  independent of every smoothing control; changing n selects a prefix.
* Evaluates the exact Gaussian kernel sum at 801 display points, instead
  of R density()'s binned FFT approximation on 512 points. Density values
  are direct evaluations; curves between grid points are drawn linearly.
* Uses shared left-closed histogram bins (last right edge included), not
  R hist()'s default right-closed bins. Exact-edge values can change bin;
  no observations are dropped. Bin bounds include floating-point edge
  cases and all bars, not just their centres, are inside the view.
* Reports KDE tail probability without renormalisation. The analytic
  population curve is likewise not renormalised to the viewport.
* Peak count uses displayed local maxima above 2% of the maximum height.
  A flat-topped maximum counts once; a shoulder is not an extra maximum.
  This is grid-, sample- and bandwidth-dependent, NOT a component-count
  estimate, uncertainty interval, hypothesis test or significance value.
  It can differ from R's approximate/coarser display.
* Seed supports the shared browser range 0 through 2147483647.

Model API
---------
model.js exports pure CommonJS/browser helpers (DensityEstimationModel):
  populationDensity(x)
    Known normalized mixture density, independent of the observations.
  validate(parameters)
    Checks seed, n, width, origin and bandwidth with Astro.ValidationError.
  prepare(seed)
    Returns {seed, values}, caching the full 1000-observation sequence.
  kdeAt(values, bandwidth, x)
    Direct normalized Gaussian sum at a finite evaluation position.
  kdeMass(values, bandwidth, lower, upper)
    Analytic KDE probability between finite ordered bounds.
  kdeOutsideMass(values, bandwidth, lower, upper)
    Analytic lower-plus-upper tail probability, evaluated stably.
  countPeaks(densities)
    Returns display-grid indices of local maxima above the 2% threshold.
  compute(parameters, prepared?)
    Returns data, parameters, values, histogram, breaks, limits, x,
    density, peaks, histogramArea and outsideMass.

Files and validation
--------------------
Runtime: index.html, model.js, app.js, plus
../TeachingTools/{statistics.js,browser.js,browser.css,lesson.js}.
No extra runtime assets. README and tests are development files only.

From the repository root, using Node 22+ and installed Chrome:
  node --test DensityEstimation/test_model.js
  env -u HTML_SITE_ROOT TMPDIR="$PWD/DensityEstimation" node DensityEstimation/test_browser.js

Numerical tests cover mixture and KDE normalisation, R-calibrated density
and CDF masses, stable tiny tails, exact histogram edge cases and area,
bin-origin periodicity, fixed observations across all smoothing controls,
bandwidth-dependent peaks/shoulders and invalid inputs. Real Chrome tests
confirm visible bin effects without data changes, all control boundaries,
undersmoothing/oversmoothing and honest tails, seed/reset reproducibility,
presets, validation, keyboard tabs, mobile layout and offline file loading.
